			<div class="content" id="post-content-4489683">
									

<p>Geez this was a ride. Its been quite awhile since a SCP messed me up like that one did. I don't know why but the stabbing test scene just f-ed me up bad. I think i finally can piece together what the whole thing is&#8230;maybe? I know like anything on this website its mostly up for the reader to decide but would love just a hint at what your thinking behind it was.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Title&nbsp;for&nbsp;hiding&nbsp;text</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>So the key points that stuck out to me about this &quot;infection&quot; were:</p>
<ul>
<li>it is something that we would consider a core aspect of life, as everyone is infected</li>
<li>once understood, it makes one see life with this aspect as repulsive (generally see how 682 sees)</li>
<li>The &quot;cure&quot; has something to do with what we would tend to see already as the negative aspects of life, with fear and pain coming immediately to mind, but &quot;being cured does seem to leave the core facets of day to day existence, meaning the foundation hasn't just purged themselves of all but their &quot;super positive delusional emotions&quot; or anything</li>
<li>The foundation still sees itself as taking on a sacrifice for humanity, as is seen in the fact that some members kill themselves understanding that they cant make that sacrifice to keep living with this knowledge, and the inversion of &quot;we die in the dark so you can live in the light&quot; to &quot;we fight in the light so you may die in the dark.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>What a some people seem to be missing is that there are to components to this event: the &quot;infection&quot;, or what truth the foundation learns, and the &quot;cure&quot; which the foundation gives themselves in order to become genocidal killers. However, they may come to the conclusion to kill everyone before the 'cure' is sent out, meaning they didn't come to the conclusion that this is what was needed because of the &quot;cures&quot; effects.<br />
If im not missing a key point, which i probably am, this all has something to do with the foundation learning that a fundamental principal of life as we know it, perhaps mortality? (and with it pain, fear, morals), is anomalous and inherently wrong. Once this is understood, it makes them appear as repulsive and unbearable, and so in order to cure it the foundation spreads some kind of form of immortality along with the information to its members who choose not to kill themselves or leave after learning. This sudden loss of pain and fear, key aspects of mortality (and morality to a degree), causes those left to not be true humans anymore, hence the absence of something in their eyes.</p>
<p>This would explain the knife test, as wounds can clearly still impact them, they can be infected, but they don't feel pain or flinch. It would also explain why Bright felt something be released from him both otherwise be unaffected, as he is already immortal but differently, thus maintaining these core aspects of humanity through either the amulet retaining them for him or him technically already being immune to the information in the first place. It also might explain why saying this truth killed those who heard it, as it is not inherently lethal to know, but some cannot handle it and commit suicide.</p>
<p>The only other thing is that there must inherently be something else wrong about existing in any regard after understanding this truth, something else that the foundation feels it must &quot;stay behind&quot; to fight against (we <strong>fight</strong> in the light (life) so you may die in the dark, as opposed to them just wanting to exterminate the &quot;repulsive&quot; humans and &quot;<strong>live</strong> in the light (full reverse of the quote), or spread the &quot;cure&quot; globally.)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Ok that's probably totally unhinged but i got stuff to do and that's the best i can do right now. hope its at least somewhat coherent.</p>

