			<div class="content" id="post-content-4468718">
									

<p>I think my main qualm with this skip is that, the ending was about a question, but the rest of the article felt like it was about an adventure and a journey. We're going to 579, and we're along for this fantastical and disturbing ride with a lot of imagery and world building, and, at least for me, I stopped feeling like we were solving a mystery and more on a mission to Mordor.</p>
<p>Now, I don't have an issue with this feeling like an adventure in a vacuum, however the way the ending plays with this adventure-vibe feels unsatisfying to me. I felt like we'd reached the destination, but we didn't get to see what the destination truly <em>was</em>. Yeah, I realize its 579 you kind of back yourself into a corner a bit. But I still wanted to see something concrete at the end. I wasn't so much looking for an answer to &quot;why?&quot;, but more an answer to &quot;what?&quot;, and the ending felt like it ignored the &quot;what?&quot; question in favor of emphasizing that you also didn't answer the &quot;why?&quot; question.</p>
<p>The build up to the ending had me, but the lack of payoff for so much build up. Not sure how exactly I'll vote but like&#8230; yeah. That's my review.</p>

